\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The attractor model of the number line (parameters fitted at 160~ms).} \textbf  {a}, Energy landscape over the internal evidence axis $x$: each integer is a well whose width grows with magnitude (well widths set by number frequency $f_n$, orange curve), so small numbers sit in deep, isolated basins that clean up to an exact value, whereas beyond $n_{\mathrm  {crit}}$ (dotted line, where adjacent wells reach $\sigma _n = 1$) the wells merge into a shallow, approximate code. \textbf  {b}, Expected readout entropy $E[H]$ per presented numerosity, before (grey) and after (black) clean-up, with the attractor's expected convergence time $E[T]$ (blue, right axis); insets show example post-clean-up posteriors. Clean-up removes the residual uncertainty of the small-$n$ readout at the cost of a few settling steps; near the boundary the settling time grows and the residual entropy rises. \textbf  {c}, The resulting response time, $\mathrm  {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T] + \kappa \,E[H]$, decomposed into its basal, convergence-time, and readout-uncertainty components.}}{3}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:conceptual}{{1}{3}{\textbf {The attractor model of the number line (parameters fitted at 160~ms).} \textbf {a}, Energy landscape over the internal evidence axis $x$: each integer is a well whose width grows with magnitude (well widths set by number frequency $f_n$, orange curve), so small numbers sit in deep, isolated basins that clean up to an exact value, whereas beyond $n_{\mathrm {crit}}$ (dotted line, where adjacent wells reach $\sigma _n = 1$) the wells merge into a shallow, approximate code. \textbf {b}, Expected readout entropy $E[H]$ per presented numerosity, before (grey) and after (black) clean-up, with the attractor's expected convergence time $E[T]$ (blue, right axis); insets show example post-clean-up posteriors. Clean-up removes the residual uncertainty of the small-$n$ readout at the cost of a few settling steps; near the boundary the settling time grows and the residual entropy rises. \textbf {c}, The resulting response time, $\mathrm {RT} = t_0 + \tau \,E[T] + \kappa \,E[H]$, decomposed into its basal, convergence-time, and readout-uncertainty components}{figure.caption.4}{}}
